        <p>
          The recursion tree of a call on $n$ elements is the rooted binary
          tree whose nodes are the invocations of <code>Merge-Sort</code>, each
          labeled by the size of its subarray. A node of size $1$ is a leaf; a
          node of size $s \ge 2$ has children of sizes <M
            >{tex`\lceil s/2 \rceil`}</M
          > and <M>{tex`\lfloor s/2 \rfloor`}</M>, since $q - p + 1 = \lfloor (p+r)/2
          \rfloor - p + 1 = \lceil s/2 \rceil$ and $r - q = \lfloor s/2 \rfloor$.
          For bookkeeping we let a leaf persist at every greater depth, so that
          "the nodes at depth $k$" is always a partition of the original array.
        </p>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 4 (Shape of the recursion tree).</strong> For every
          $k \ge 0$, every node at depth $k$ has size either <M
            >{tex`\lfloor n/2^k \rfloor`}</M
          > or <M>{tex`\lceil n/2^k \rceil`}</M>, the sizes of the depth-$k$ nodes
          sum to exactly $n$, and the number of depth-$k$ nodes is at most <M
            >{tex`\min(2^k, n)`}</M
          >. Moreover the depth of the tree is exactly <M
            >{tex`\lceil \log_2 n \rceil`}</M
          >.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> Induction on $k$. For $k=0$
            the single node has size <M>{tex`n = \lfloor n/2^0 \rfloor`}</M>.
            Assume the claim at depth $k$ and let a node there have size $s$
            with <M>{tex`\lfloor n/2^k \rfloor \le s \le \lceil n/2^k \rceil`}</M>.
            Its children have sizes <M>{tex`\lfloor s/2 \rfloor`}</M> and <M
              >{tex`\lceil s/2 \rceil`}</M
            >. Monotonicity of $x \mapsto \lfloor x/2 \rfloor$ together with
            Lemma 2 gives
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}\left\lfloor \frac{s}{2} \right\rfloor \ge \left\lfloor \frac{\lfloor n/2^k \rfloor}{2} \right\rfloor = \left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{k+1}} \right\rfloor, \qquad \left\lceil \frac{s}{2} \right\rceil \le \left\lceil \frac{\lceil n/2^k \rceil}{2} \right\rceil = \left\lceil \frac{n}{2^{k+1}} \right\rceil.\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            Both child sizes therefore lie in the interval <M
              >{tex`[\lfloor n/2^{k+1} \rfloor, \lceil n/2^{k+1} \rceil]`}</M
            >, whose endpoints differ by at most $1$ and which consequently
            contains no integer other than those two endpoints. That is the
            first claim. The sizes still sum to $n$ because a split of a node of
            size $s$ produces children of total size <M
              >{tex`\lfloor s/2 \rfloor + \lceil s/2 \rceil = s`}</M
            >, and a persisting leaf contributes its own size unchanged; the
            node count at most doubles per level and cannot exceed $n$ because
            the sizes are positive integers summing to $n$.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the depth: all depth-$k$ nodes are leaves precisely when the
            largest of them has size $1$, i.e. when <M
              >{tex`\lceil n/2^k \rceil = 1`}</M
            >, which holds if and only if <M>{tex`n \le 2^k`}</M>, i.e. if and
            only if <M>{tex`k \ge \log_2 n`}</M>. The least such integer $k$ is
            <M>{tex`\lceil \log_2 n \rceil`}</M>, and for every smaller $k$ the
            bound <M>{tex`\lceil n/2^k \rceil \ge 2`}</M> shows some node still
            splits. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <Theorem name="Running time of merge sort">
          Let $T(n)$ be the worst-case running time of <code>Merge-Sort</code>
          on $n$ elements. Then <M>{tex`T(n) \in \Theta(n \log n)`}</M>;
          explicitly, with <M>{tex`a_1, a_2`}</M> as in Lemma 3 and $b$ the
          constant cost of the non-merging part of one invocation,
          <E
            >{tex`\begin{equation}\frac{a_1}{2}\, n \log_2 n \;\le\; T(n) \;\le\; 2(a_2 + b)\, n \log_2 n \qquad \text{for all } n \ge 4.\end{equation}`}</E
          >
        </Theorem>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span>
            <em>Upper bound.</em> The total running time is the sum, over all
            nodes of the recursion tree, of the work done at that node: at most
            <M>{tex`a_2 s + b`}</M> for a node of size $s$, by Lemma 3, where
            $b$ bounds the constant cost of the test, the computation of $q$,
            and the two calls. Group the nodes by depth. By Lemma 4 the sizes at
            depth $k$ sum to $n$ and the number of nodes at that depth is at
            most $n$, so the work at depth $k$ is at most <M
              >{tex`a_2 n + b n = (a_2+b)n`}</M
            >. There are <M>{tex`\lceil \log_2 n \rceil + 1`}</M> depths, whence
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}T(n) \;\le\; (a_2+b)\, n \left( \lceil \log_2 n \rceil + 1 \right) \;\le\; (a_2+b)\, n \left( \log_2 n + 2 \right) \;\le\; 2(a_2+b)\, n \log_2 n,\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            the last step because <M>{tex`\log_2 n \ge 2`}</M> for $n \ge 4$, so
            that <M>{tex`\log_2 n + 2 \le 2 \log_2 n`}</M>. This is the
            definition of <M>{tex`T(n) \in O(n \log n)`}</M> with <M
              >{tex`M = 2(a_2+b)`}</M
            > and <M>{tex`n_0 = 4`}</M>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Lower bound.</em> Fix any $k$ with <M
              >{tex`0 \le k \le \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor - 1`}</M
            >. Then <M>{tex`2^k \le 2^{\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor - 1} \le n/2`}</M>,
            so every node at depth $k$ has size at least <M
              >{tex`\lfloor n/2^k \rfloor \ge 2`}</M
            > and is therefore an internal node that really executes a merge. By
            Lemma 3 that merge costs at least <M>{tex`a_1`}</M> times the node's
            size, and by Lemma 4 those sizes sum to exactly $n$, so depth $k$
            contributes at least <M>{tex`a_1 n`}</M>. Since this holds for
            <M>{tex`\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor`}</M> distinct depths and all work
            is non-negative,
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}T(n) \;\ge\; a_1 n \left\lfloor \log_2 n \right\rfloor \;\ge\; a_1 n \left( \log_2 n - 1 \right) \;\ge\; \frac{a_1}{2}\, n \log_2 n \qquad (n \ge 4).\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            Note that this bound holds for <em>every</em> input of size $n$,
            which is stronger than the $\Omega$ that the definition requires.
            Combining the two displays gives <M
              >{tex`T(n) \in \Theta(n\log n)`}</M
            >. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          It is instructive to see the same answer come out of the recurrence
          itself. Writing the recursion of Lemma 3 and Lemma 4 as an inequality
          between numbers rather than a sum over a tree, we have $T(1) = d$ and
        </p>
        <E>{tex`\begin{equation}T(n) \;=\; T\!\left(\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rceil\right) + T\!\left(\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor\right) + \Theta(n),\end{equation}`}</E>
        <p>
          which for $n$ a power of two is the clean recurrence
          <M>{tex`T(n) = 2T(n/2) + cn`}</M>, and it can be solved exactly.
        </p>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 5 (Exact solution at powers of two).</strong> If
          $T(1) = d$ and <M>{tex`T(n) = 2T(n/2) + cn`}</M> for every even $n$,
          then <M>{tex`T(2^m) = c\, m\, 2^m + d\, 2^m`}</M> for all integers
          $m \ge 0$.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> Induction on $m$. For $m=0$
            the formula reads <M>{tex`T(1) = 0 + d`}</M>, which is the base
            case. Assuming it for $m$,
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{align*} T(2^{m+1}) &= 2\,T(2^m) + c\,2^{m+1} = 2\left( c\,m\,2^m + d\,2^m \right) + c\,2^{m+1} \\ &= c\,m\,2^{m+1} + c\,2^{m+1} + d\,2^{m+1} = c\,(m+1)\,2^{m+1} + d\,2^{m+1}, \end{align*}`}</E
            >
            which is the formula for $m+1$. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          With $n = 2^m$, i.e. <M>{tex`m = \log_2 n`}</M>, this says
          <M>{tex`T(n) = c\,n\log_2 n + d\,n \in \Theta(n \log n)`}</M>, in
          agreement with Theorem 1. The same conclusion follows from the Master
          Theorem: here $a = 2$, $b = 2$, so <M>{tex`\log_b a = 1`}</M> and
          <M>{tex`f(n) = \Theta(n) = \Theta(n^{\log_b a} \log^0 n)`}</M>, which
          is Case 2 with $k = 0$ and yields <M
            >{tex`T(n) \in \Theta(n^{1} \log^{1} n)`}</M
          >. The tree argument is the honest version of that citation: it is
          what the proof of the Master Theorem does in this special case, and it
          is what justifies ignoring the floors and ceilings.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Optimality.</strong> The <M>{tex`n\log n`}</M> is not an
          artifact of merge sort. Any deterministic algorithm that sorts by
          comparing keys can be drawn as a binary decision tree whose internal
          nodes are comparisons and whose leaves are the possible output
          permutations; it must have at least $n!$ leaves, since two distinct
          permutations reaching the same leaf would be sorted identically and at
          most one of the two outputs can be correct. A binary tree of height
          $h$ has at most <M>{tex`2^h`}</M> leaves (immediate induction on $h$),
          so <M>{tex`2^h \ge n!`}</M> and, by Stirling's bound <M
            >{tex`n! \ge (n/e)^n`}</M
          >,
          <E
            >{tex`\begin{equation}h \;\ge\; \log_2 (n!) \;\ge\; n \log_2 n - n \log_2 e \;\in\; \Omega(n \log n).\end{equation}`}</E
          >
          Merge sort is therefore asymptotically optimal among comparison
          sorts.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Space.</strong> The auxiliary space is $\Theta(n)$, and the
          bound is tight for the reason one might not expect: although the
          recursion is $\Theta(\log n)$ deep and each frame allocates a buffer,
          the buffers are never simultaneously live. A node's <code>Merge</code>
          runs only after both of its recursive calls have returned and released
          their buffers, so the buffers alive at any instant lie on a single
          root-to-node path with only the deepest one in use; the peak is
          therefore the largest single buffer, $\Theta(n)$, at the root's merge.
          Adding the $\Theta(\log n)$ words of stack (one frame per level, by
          Lemma 4) gives $\Theta(n)$ in total.
        </p>
      </section> -->

      <section id="example-binary-search">
        <h3>Example 1: Binary Search</h3>
        <p>
          Binary search looks for a key $x$ in a sorted array
          $A[1 \ldots n]$. The claim "it halves the interval, therefore it is
          logarithmic" is the right intuition but not a proof: the interval is
          not halved, it is reduced to <M>{tex`\lfloor (s-1)/2 \rfloor`}</M> or
          <M>{tex`\lceil (s-1)/2 \rceil`}</M>, and one has to check that this
          still forces $\Theta(\log n)$ from both sides.
        </p>
  <Sample>Binary-Search(A, n, x) // A[1..n] sorted nondecreasingly
lo &lt;- 1;  hi &lt;- n
while lo &lt;= hi do
  mid &lt;- floor((lo + hi) / 2)
  if   A[mid] = x then return mid
  elif A[mid] &lt; x then lo &lt;- mid + 1
  else                 hi &lt;- mid - 1
return not-found</Sample>

        <p>
          Write <M>{tex`s_k = \mathit{hi} - \mathit{lo} + 1`}</M> for the number of candidate
          positions still under consideration after $k$ completed iterations,
          so that <M>{tex`s_0 = n`}</M>, and observe that the loop guard
          <M>{tex`\mathit{lo} \le \mathit{hi}`}</M> is exactly the condition <M
            >{tex`s_k \ge 1`}</M
          >.
        </p>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 6 (One iteration).</strong> Suppose iteration $k+1$ is
          entered (so <M>{tex`s_k \ge 1`}</M>) and does not return. Then
          <E
            >{tex`\begin{equation}s_{k+1} \in \left\{ \left\lfloor \frac{s_k - 1}{2} \right\rfloor,\; \left\lceil \frac{s_k - 1}{2} \right\rceil \right\}, \qquad\text{hence}\qquad \frac{s_k - 2}{2} \;\le\; s_{k+1} \;\le\; \frac{s_k}{2}.\end{equation}`}</E
          >
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> Put $s = s_k$ and write
            <M>{tex`\mathit{mid} = \lfloor (\mathit{lo}+\mathit{hi})/2 \rfloor`}</M>. If the algorithm
            takes the branch <M>{tex`\mathit{lo} \leftarrow \mathit{mid}+1`}</M>, the new count is
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}\mathit{hi} - \mathit{mid} = \mathit{hi} - \left\lfloor \frac{\mathit{lo}+\mathit{hi}}{2} \right\rfloor = \left\lceil \frac{\mathit{hi}-\mathit{lo}}{2} \right\rceil = \left\lceil \frac{s-1}{2} \right\rceil,\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            and if it takes the branch <M>{tex`\mathit{hi} \leftarrow \mathit{mid}-1`}</M> it is
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}\mathit{mid} - \mathit{lo} = \left\lfloor \frac{\mathit{lo}+\mathit{hi}}{2} \right\rfloor - \mathit{lo} = \left\lfloor \frac{\mathit{hi}-\mathit{lo}}{2} \right\rfloor = \left\lfloor \frac{s-1}{2} \right\rfloor.\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            For the numeric bounds: <M
              >{tex`\lceil (s-1)/2 \rceil \le s/2`}</M
            > holds because for even $s$ the left side is $s/2$ exactly, and for
            odd $s$ it equals $(s-1)/2 &lt; s/2$; and <M
              >{tex`\lfloor (s-1)/2 \rfloor \ge (s-2)/2`}</M
            > because <M>{tex`\lfloor y \rfloor > y-1`}</M>. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <Theorem name="Running time of binary search">
          The worst-case running time of <code>Binary-Search</code> satisfies
          <M>{tex`T(n) \in \Theta(\log n)`}</M>. More precisely, the loop
          executes at most <M>{tex`\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1`}</M>
          iterations on every input, and at least <M
            >{tex`\log_2 n - 2`}</M
          > iterations on the input where $x$ is smaller than every element of
          $A$.
        </Theorem>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span>
            <em>Upper bound.</em> We claim <M>{tex`s_k \le n/2^k`}</M> for every
            $k$ at which the loop is still running. This is an induction: <M
              >{tex`s_0 = n`}</M
            >, and if <M>{tex`s_k \le n/2^k`}</M> then by Lemma 6 <M
              >{tex`s_{k+1} \le s_k/2 \le n/2^{k+1}`}</M
            >. Now iteration $k+1$ is entered only if <M>{tex`s_k \ge 1`}</M>,
            which forces <M>{tex`n/2^k \ge 1`}</M>, i.e. <M
              >{tex`k \le \log_2 n`}</M
            >. The iterations are therefore indexed by <M
              >{tex`k = 0, 1, \ldots, \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor`}</M
            >, at most <M>{tex`\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1`}</M> of them. Each
            iteration executes a fixed number of primitive operations (one
            addition, one halving, one array read and at most two comparisons
            and one assignment), say at most $c$; adding the constant $d$ for
            initialization and the final return,
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}T(n) \;\le\; c\left( \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1 \right) + d \;\le\; 2c \log_2 n \quad\text{for } n \ge 2^{(c+d)/c},\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            so <M>{tex`T(n) \in O(\log n)`}</M>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Lower bound.</em> Take the input in which $x$ is strictly
            smaller than every $A[i]$. Then no comparison can report equality,
            the algorithm never returns early, and every iteration takes the
            branch <M>{tex`\mathit{hi} \leftarrow \mathit{mid}-1`}</M>, so by Lemma 6 <M
              >{tex`s_{k+1} = \lfloor (s_k-1)/2 \rfloor \ge (s_k-2)/2`}</M
            >. We claim <M>{tex`s_k \ge n/2^k - 2`}</M> for every $k$ reached.
            Indeed <M>{tex`s_0 = n \ge n - 2`}</M>, and if the claim holds at
            $k$ then
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}s_{k+1} \;\ge\; \frac{s_k - 2}{2} \;\ge\; \frac{n/2^k - 4}{2} \;=\; \frac{n}{2^{k+1}} - 2.\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            Consequently <M>{tex`s_k \ge 1`}</M>, and hence another iteration
            runs, whenever <M>{tex`n/2^k \ge 3`}</M>, that is whenever <M
              >{tex`k \le \log_2 n - \log_2 3`}</M
            >. The number of iterations on this input is therefore at least
            <M>{tex`\log_2 n - \log_2 3 \ge \log_2 n - 2`}</M>, and since each
            iteration costs at least one primitive operation, <M
              >{tex`T(n) \ge \log_2 n - 2 \ge \tfrac12 \log_2 n`}</M
            > for $n \ge 16$. With the upper bound this gives <M
              >{tex`T(n) \in \Theta(\log n)`}</M
            >. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Solving the recurrence gives the same answer with less information:
          the recursive form obeys <M
            >{tex`T(n) = T(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor) + \Theta(1)`}</M
          >, which is the Master Theorem with $a=1$, $b=2$, <M
            >{tex`\log_b a = 0`}</M
          > and <M>{tex`f(n) = \Theta(1) = \Theta(n^0 \log^0 n)`}</M>: Case 2
          with $k=0$, giving <M>{tex`T(n) \in \Theta(\log n)`}</M>. Unrolling by
          hand is just as quick: <M
            >{tex`T(n) = T(n/2^k) + \Theta(k)`}</M
          > and the recursion bottoms out at <M>{tex`k = \Theta(\log n)`}</M>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Optimality.</strong> No comparison-based search can do
          better, and the proof is a counting argument rather than an analysis
          of any particular algorithm.
        </p>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 7 (Leaves of a binary tree).</strong> A binary tree of
          height $h$ has at most <M>{tex`2^h`}</M> leaves.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> Induction on $h$. A tree of
            height $0$ is a single node, which is one leaf, and <M
              >{tex`2^0 = 1`}</M
            >. A tree of height $h \ge 1$ has a root with at most two subtrees,
            each of height at most $h-1$; every leaf of the tree is a leaf of
            one of the subtrees, so the leaf count is at most <M
              >{tex`2 \cdot 2^{h-1} = 2^h`}</M
            >. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <Theorem name="Lower bound for comparison-based search">
          Let <M>{tex`\mathcal{A}`}</M> be any deterministic algorithm that,
          given a sorted array of $n$ distinct keys and a key $x$ not present in
          the array, determines the position at which $x$ would have to be
          inserted, using only comparisons between $x$ and array entries. Then
          <M>{tex`\mathcal{A}`}</M> performs at least <M
            >{tex`\lceil \log_2 (n+1) \rceil`}</M
          > comparisons on some input.
        </Theorem>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> Because $x$ is absent, every
            comparison $x$ versus $A[i]$ has exactly two possible outcomes, so
            the execution of <M>{tex`\mathcal{A}`}</M> on the family of such
            inputs is described by a binary tree: each internal node is the
            comparison the algorithm performs next (determined by the outcomes
            so far, since <M>{tex`\mathcal{A}`}</M> is deterministic), its two
            children are the two outcomes, and each leaf carries the answer
            output there. There are $n+1$ possible answers, one for each gap
            between consecutive elements including the two ends, and every one
            of them occurs for some input. Two different answers cannot share a
            leaf, since all inputs reaching a leaf receive the same output;
            hence the tree has at least $n+1$ leaves, and by Lemma 7 its height
            $h$ satisfies <M>{tex`2^h \ge n+1`}</M>, i.e. <M
              >{tex`h \ge \lceil \log_2(n+1) \rceil`}</M
            >. A root-to-leaf path of that length is an input on which <M
              >{tex`\mathcal{A}`}</M
            > makes that many comparisons. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Binary search performs at most <M
            >{tex`\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1`}</M
          > comparisons, so it is optimal up to an additive constant.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Space.</strong> The iterative version keeps only <M
            >{tex`\mathit{lo}`}</M
          >, <M>{tex`\mathit{hi}`}</M> and <M>{tex`\mathit{mid}`}</M>, so its auxiliary space is
          $\Theta(1)$. The recursive version pushes one $O(1)$-sized frame per
          iteration and, by the theorem above, makes at most <M
            >{tex`\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1`}</M
          > of them before returning, so its stack usage is $\Theta(\log n)$ in
          the worst case, unless the compiler eliminates the tail call, in
          which case it is again $\Theta(1)$.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="example-topological-sort">
        <h3>Example 2: Topological Sort</h3>
        <p>
          Let $G = (V,E)$ be a directed graph given by adjacency lists, and
          write $V$ and $E$ for both the sets and their cardinalities, as is
          customary. A topological order is a linear order of the vertices in
          which every edge points forward. The statement "each vertex and each
          edge is visited a constant number of times" is the whole content of
          the $\Theta(V+E)$ bound, so it is precisely that statement which has
          to be proved, and it is not obvious, because a vertex could in
          principle be pushed onto the queue once per incoming edge.
        </p>
        <Sample>Kahn(G)
  for each v in V do indeg[v] &lt;- 0                      // pass 1
  for each u in V do                                    // pass 2
      for each v in Adj[u] do indeg[v] &lt;- indeg[v] + 1
  S &lt;- empty queue                                      // pass 3
  for each v in V do
      if indeg[v] = 0 then Enqueue(S, v)
  L &lt;- empty list;  count &lt;- 0
  while S is not empty do                               // main loop
      u &lt;- Dequeue(S);  append u to L;  count &lt;- count + 1
      for each v in Adj[u] do
          indeg[v] &lt;- indeg[v] - 1
          if indeg[v] = 0 then Enqueue(S, v)
  if count &lt; |V| then report "G has a cycle" else return L</Sample>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 8 (Each vertex is enqueued at most once).</strong> Over
          the whole execution, <code>Enqueue(S, v)</code> is executed at most
          once for each vertex $v$.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> After pass 2 the counter
            <code>indeg[v]</code> holds <M>{tex`\deg^-(v)`}</M>, and from that
            moment on it is only ever decremented; it is therefore a strictly
            decreasing function of time on the (finite) set of instants at which
            it changes. A strictly decreasing integer-valued function takes the
            value $0$ at most once, so there is at most one instant $t_v$ at
            which <M>{tex`\mathit{indeg}[v] = 0`}</M> holds and can be observed. Every
            enqueue of $v$ is guarded by a test <M>{tex`\mathit{indeg}[v] = 0`}</M> (in
            pass 3 if <M>{tex`\deg^-(v) = 0`}</M> already, and in the main loop
            immediately after a decrement); hence every enqueue of $v$ happens
            at $t_v$, and the tests in question occur at most once each at that
            instant. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 9 (Each adjacency list is scanned at most once).</strong
          > The inner loop of the main loop scans <M>{tex`\mathit{Adj}[u]`}</M> at most once
          for each $u$; consequently the main loop performs at most
          <M>{tex`\sum_{u \in V} \deg^+(u) = E`}</M> decrements in total.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> The scan of <M
              >{tex`\mathit{Adj}[u]`}</M
            > is executed only in the iteration of the main loop that dequeues
            $u$. Since the queue is a set of enqueued items and each dequeue
            removes one item, the number of dequeues of $u$ is at most the
            number of enqueues of $u$, which is at most one by Lemma 8. The
            count of decrements follows from the handshake identity of Lemma 1.
            
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 10 (Termination with all vertices, on a DAG).</strong>
          If $G$ is acyclic, then at termination $count = |V|$; every vertex is
          enqueued and dequeued exactly once. If $G$ has a cycle, then <M
            >{tex`\mathit{count} < |V|`}</M
          >, so the final test is a correct acyclicity test.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> Suppose $G$ is acyclic but
            some vertices are never enqueued; let $W$ be the (non-empty) set of
            those vertices. The main loop runs until $S$ is empty, so every
            enqueued vertex is eventually dequeued; hence the dequeued vertices
            are exactly <M>{tex`V \setminus W`}</M>. Fix <M>{tex`v \in W`}</M>.
            Its counter is tested for $0$ after every decrement, and $v$ was
            never enqueued, so its counter was never $0$; at termination it
            equals
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}\mathit{indeg}[v] \;=\; \deg^-(v) \;-\; \#\{\, u : (u,v) \in E \text{ and } u \text{ was dequeued} \,\} \;>\; 0,\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            which means $v$ has an in-neighbor $u$ that was never dequeued,
            i.e. <M>{tex`u \in W`}</M>. So every vertex of $W$ has an
            in-neighbor inside $W$. Starting from any <M>{tex`v_0 \in W`}</M>
            and repeatedly stepping to such an in-neighbor produces an infinite
            walk <M
              >{tex`v_0 \leftarrow v_1 \leftarrow v_2 \leftarrow \cdots`}</M
            > inside the finite set $W$; by the pigeonhole principle two of the
            <M>{tex`v_i`}</M> coincide, say <M>{tex`v_i = v_j`}</M> with <M
              >{tex`i < j`}</M
            >, and then <M
              >{tex`v_j \to v_{j-1} \to \cdots \to v_i`}</M
            > is a directed cycle, contradicting acyclicity. Hence $W$ is
            empty. Conversely, if $G$ contains a cycle $C$, no vertex of $C$ can
            ever be dequeued: the first vertex of $C$ to be dequeued would have
            had its counter reach $0$, yet its predecessor on $C$ had not been
            dequeued at that time and so had not contributed its decrement, and
            the counter never dips below the number of undecremented in-edges.
            Therefore <M>{tex`\mathit{count} \le |V| - |C| < |V|`}</M>. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <Theorem name="Running time of Kahn's algorithm">
          <code>Kahn</code> runs in time $\Theta(V+E)$ on every input, in the
          adjacency-list representation.
        </Theorem>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span>
            <em>Upper bound.</em> Pass 1 and pass 3 execute $O(1)$ work per
            vertex, so $O(V)$ each. Pass 2 executes $O(1)$ work per adjacency
            entry, so by Lemma 1 it costs <M
              >{tex`O\!\left(V + \sum_u \deg^+(u)\right) = O(V+E)`}</M
            >, the $V$ accounting for the per-vertex loop overhead even at
            vertices with empty lists. In the main loop, the work of the
            iteration that dequeues $u$ is <M
              >{tex`O(1) + O(\deg^+(u))`}</M
            >: a dequeue, an append, an increment, and one pass over <M
              >{tex`\mathit{Adj}[u]`}</M
            > doing $O(1)$ work per entry. Each vertex is dequeued at most once
            (Lemma 9), so summing over the dequeued vertices and applying Lemma
            1 again bounds the main loop by
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}\sum_{u \in V} \left( c_1 + c_2 \deg^+(u) \right) \;=\; c_1 V + c_2 E.\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            Adding the passes gives <M>{tex`T \le M(V+E)`}</M> for a constant
            $M$.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Lower bound.</em> Passes 1–3 are executed unconditionally and
            already touch all $V$ vertices and all $E$ adjacency entries, at a
            cost of at least one primitive operation each, so <M
              >{tex`T \ge V + E`}</M
            > on every input. Hence <M>{tex`T \in \Theta(V+E)`}</M>.
            
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          <strong>Remark (why no algorithm can do better).</strong> The
          theorem is a statement about this algorithm; that the
          <em>problem</em> needs $\Omega(V+E)$ is a separate, adversary-style
          argument. The $\Omega(V)$ half is immediate, since the output has $V$
          entries. For the $\Omega(E)$ half, fix $q$ and let <M
            >{tex`G_0`}</M
          > be the graph on <M>{tex`a_1,\ldots,a_q,\, b_1,\ldots,b_q`}</M> with
          all <M>{tex`q^2`}</M> edges <M>{tex`(a_i, b_j)`}</M>; it is acyclic,
          as no edge leaves a $b$. For each edge $e$, let <M>{tex`G_e`}</M> be
          the same graph with $e$ reversed; <M>{tex`G_e`}</M> is acyclic too,
          because a cycle would have to leave <M>{tex`b_j`}</M> along the single
          reversed edge and return to it, and the only edges into <M
            >{tex`b_j`}</M
          > come from the $a$'s, which have no other way back. Now suppose a
          deterministic algorithm halts on <M>{tex`G_0`}</M> without ever
          inspecting the adjacency entry for $e = (a_i, b_j)$. Run it on <M
            >{tex`G_e`}</M
          >: every value it reads is identical, so it produces the same order.
          That order places <M>{tex`a_i`}</M> before <M>{tex`b_j`}</M> or the
          other way round, and is therefore wrong on one of the two acyclic
          inputs. Hence on <M>{tex`G_0`}</M> the algorithm inspects all <M
            >{tex`q^2`}</M
          > entries, which is $\Omega(E)$ with $V = 2q$; padding with isolated
          vertices and thinning the bipartite core tunes $V$ and $E$
          independently. Kahn's algorithm is therefore optimal to within a
          constant factor.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The DFS formulation.</strong> The alternative algorithm runs
          a depth-first search over all vertices and prepends each vertex to a
          list when it is finished. Its cost is $\Theta(V+E)$ for the same
          reason in a different disguise: <code>DFS-Visit(u)</code> is called
          only when $u$ is white and colors it gray on entry, and colors are
          never reset, so it is called at most once per vertex; it then scans
          <M>{tex`\mathit{Adj}[u]`}</M> exactly once, doing $O(1)$ work per entry, and
          the outer loop contributes $O(V)$. Summing with Lemma 1 gives
          $O(V+E)$, and the outer loop's unconditional pass over $V$ plus the
          scan of every adjacency list gives $\Omega(V+E)$. The order produced
          is correct because for every edge $(u,v)$ one has $f(v) &lt; f(u)$ for
          the finishing times: when the edge is explored $u$ is gray, and $v$ is
          then either white, in which case it becomes a descendant of $u$ and
          finishes first, or black, so it has already finished, or gray, which would
          make $(u,v)$ a back edge and produce a cycle, impossible in a DAG.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Space.</strong> Both formulations use $\Theta(V)$ auxiliary
          space (the counters and queue for Kahn, the colors and the
          recursion stack of depth at most $V$ for DFS) on top of the
          $\Theta(V+E)$ occupied by the input itself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="example-dijkstra">
        <h3>Example 3: Dijkstra's Algorithm</h3>
        <p>
          Dijkstra's algorithm computes single-source shortest paths in a
          directed graph with non-negative edge weights <M
            >{tex`w : E \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}`}</M
          >. Its running time is not a property of the algorithm alone: it is
          the number of priority-queue operations, which depends only on the
          graph, multiplied by the cost of those operations, which depends only
          on the queue. Separating the two is the whole analysis, and it is what
          makes the array, binary-heap and Fibonacci-heap variants three
          corollaries of one theorem instead of three separate arguments.
        </p>
        <Sample>Dijkstra(G, w, s)
  for each v in V do d[v] &lt;- infinity;  parent[v] &lt;- nil;  done[v] &lt;- false
  d[s] &lt;- 0
  Q &lt;- priority queue containing every v in V, keyed by d[v]
  while Q is not empty do
      u &lt;- Extract-Min(Q)
      done[u] &lt;- true
      for each v in Adj[u] do                          // relax edge (u,v)
          if not done[v] and d[u] + w(u,v) &lt; d[v] then
              d[v] &lt;- d[u] + w(u,v)
              parent[v] &lt;- u
              Decrease-Key(Q, v, d[v])</Sample>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 11 (Operation counts).</strong> On a graph with $V$
          vertices and $E$ edges, one execution of <code>Dijkstra</code>
          performs exactly $V$ <code>Extract-Min</code> operations, at most $E$
          <code>Decrease-Key</code> operations, one queue construction of $V$
          elements, and $\Theta(V+E)$ additional primitive operations outside
          the queue.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> No element is ever inserted
            after the queue is built, and each iteration of the main loop
            removes exactly one element, so the loop runs exactly $V$ times and
            performs exactly $V$ extractions; in particular every vertex is
            extracted exactly once. The scan of <M>{tex`\mathit{Adj}[u]`}</M> occurs only
            in the iteration that extracts $u$, hence at most once per vertex,
            so each edge $(u,v)$ is relaxed at most once and each relaxation
            issues at most one <code>Decrease-Key</code>. By Lemma 1 the number
            of relaxations is <M>{tex`\sum_u \deg^+(u) = E`}</M>, bounding the
            decrease-keys by $E$. Outside the queue, the initialization is
            $\Theta(V)$, the main loop's per-iteration bookkeeping is $O(1)$,
            and the adjacency scans cost $\Theta(1)$ per entry, $\Theta(E)$ in
            total; the sum is $\Theta(V+E)$. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Note what this proof did <em>not</em> use: non-negativity of the
          weights. The counting holds because of the <code>done</code> flag,
          which lets a vertex be extracted only once. Non-negativity is what
          makes that safe: it is needed for correctness, to guarantee that
          <M>{tex`d[u]`}</M> is already final when $u$ is extracted. A
          "lazy" implementation that reinserts vertices instead of decreasing
          keys can, on graphs with negative edges, perform exponentially many
          extractions. Correctness and the running-time bound are entangled
          here, not independent.
        </p>

        <div class="lemma">
          <strong>Lemma 12 (Binary heap operations).</strong> A binary
          min-heap holding $h$ elements in a complete binary tree has height
          <M>{tex`\lfloor \log_2 h \rfloor`}</M>, and <code>Insert</code>,
          <code>Extract-Min</code> and <code>Decrease-Key</code> each run in
          <M>{tex`O(\log h)`}</M> time; building a heap of $h$ elements costs
          $\Theta(h)$.
        </div>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> A complete binary tree of
            height $\eta$ has all levels <M>{tex`0, \ldots, \eta-1`}</M> full
            and level $\eta$ non-empty, so its size $h$ satisfies <M
              >{tex`2^{\eta} \le h \le 2^{\eta+1} - 1`}</M
            >, whence <M>{tex`\eta \le \log_2 h < \eta + 1`}</M> and <M
              >{tex`\eta = \lfloor \log_2 h \rfloor`}</M
            >. <code>Sift-Up</code> compares an element with its parent and, if
            they are out of order, swaps and repeats one level higher; each
            iteration strictly decreases the element's depth, so there are at
            most $\eta$ iterations of $O(1)$ work each. <code>Sift-Down</code>
            likewise strictly increases the depth by one per iteration. Since
            <code>Insert</code> is an append followed by a
            <code>Sift-Up</code>, <code>Decrease-Key</code> is a key update
            followed by a <code>Sift-Up</code> (the heap property can only be
            violated upwards), and <code>Extract-Min</code> moves the last
            element to the root and calls <code>Sift-Down</code>, all three cost
            <M>{tex`O(\eta) = O(\log h)`}</M>. For the construction, sifting
            down every node from the bottom up costs at most
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}\sum_{\ell=0}^{\eta} \left\lceil \frac{h}{2^{\ell+1}} \right\rceil O(\eta - \ell) \;=\; O\!\left( h \sum_{i=0}^{\infty} \frac{i}{2^{i}} \right) \;=\; O(2h) \;=\; O(h),\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            using that level $\ell$ holds at most <M
              >{tex`\lceil h/2^{\ell+1} \rceil`}</M
            > nodes whose sift-down travels at most <M>{tex`\eta - \ell`}</M>
            levels, and that <M>{tex`\sum_{i \ge 0} i/2^i = 2`}</M>. It is also
            $\Omega(h)$ since every element is touched. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <Theorem name="Running time of Dijkstra's algorithm">
          With adjacency lists, <code>Dijkstra</code> runs in time
          <E
            >{tex`\begin{equation}T \;=\; \Theta(V+E) \;+\; V \cdot C_{\text{ext}} \;+\; O(E) \cdot C_{\text{dec}} \;+\; C_{\text{build}},\end{equation}`}</E
          >
          where <M>{tex`C_{\text{ext}}, C_{\text{dec}}, C_{\text{build}}`}</M>
          are the costs of an extract-min, a decrease-key and building the queue.
          In particular:
          <ul style="line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            <li class="bullet">
              binary heap: <M>{tex`T \in O\big((V+E)\log V\big)`}</M>;
            </li>
            <li class="bullet">
              unsorted array: <M>{tex`T \in \Theta(V^2 + E) = \Theta(V^2)`}</M>;
            </li>
            <li class="bullet">
              Fibonacci heap: <M>{tex`T \in O\big(E + V \log V\big)`}</M>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </Theorem>

        <div class="proof">
          <p>
            <span class="proof-label">Proof.</span> The displayed identity is
            Lemma 11 together with the observation that the algorithm's work
            splits into queue operations and everything else, and that the costs
            are additive. The three cases are then substitutions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Binary heap.</em> The queue never holds more than $V$ elements,
            so by Lemma 12 <M
              >{tex`C_{\text{ext}}, C_{\text{dec}} \in O(\log V)`}</M
            > and <M>{tex`C_{\text{build}} = \Theta(V)`}</M>. Substituting,
            <E
              >{tex`\begin{equation}T \;\in\; \Theta(V+E) + O(V \log V) + O(E \log V) + \Theta(V) \;=\; O\big((V+E)\log V\big).\end{equation}`}</E
            >
            If every vertex is reachable from $s$ then <M
              >{tex`E \ge V-1`}</M
            > and this simplifies to <M>{tex`O(E \log V)`}</M>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Unsorted array.</em> Take $Q$ to be an array of $V$ keys.
            Building it is $\Theta(V)$; an extract-min is a linear scan,
            <M>{tex`C_{\text{ext}} = \Theta(V)`}</M>; a decrease-key is a single
            write, <M>{tex`C_{\text{dec}} = \Theta(1)`}</M>. Hence
            <M>{tex`T = \Theta(V+E) + V \cdot \Theta(V) + O(E) = \Theta(V^2+E)`}</M>,
            and since <M>{tex`E \le V^2`}</M> this is <M
              >{tex`\Theta(V^2)`}</M
            >. Unlike the heap bound, this one is a genuine $\Theta$: the $V$
            linear scans are performed unconditionally.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Fibonacci heap.</em> Fredman and Tarjan's structure supports
            insert and decrease-key in $O(1)$ amortized time and extract-min in
            <M>{tex`O(\log h)`}</M> amortized time, meaning that any sequence of
            $\alpha$ inserts, $\beta$ decrease-keys and $\gamma$ extract-mins on
            a heap of size at most $h$ costs <M
              >{tex`O(\alpha + \beta + \gamma \log h)`}</M
            > in total, even though an individual operation may cost more. With
            <M>{tex`\alpha = V`}</M>, <M>{tex`\beta \le E`}</M>, <M
              >{tex`\gamma = V`}</M
            > and $h \le V$, the queue's total contribution is <M
              >{tex`O(V + E + V \log V)`}</M
            >, and adding the $\Theta(V+E)$ of Lemma 11 gives <M
              >{tex`O(E + V\log V)`}</M
            >. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          <strong>Why $O$ and not $\Theta$ for the heap variant.</strong> The
          bound <M>{tex`\Theta((V+E)\log V)`}</M> is quoted so often that it is
          worth saying exactly why it is an abuse of notation. Lemma 11 bounds
          the number of decrease-keys by $E$, but on many inputs almost none of
          them fire: if the edges are scanned in an order that never improves a
          tentative distance, the cost is <M>{tex`\Theta(V \log V + E)`}</M>,
          which is smaller. And an individual heap operation costs
          <M>{tex`\Theta(\log V)`}</M> only in the worst case; a sift-up that
          stops immediately costs $O(1)$. So $O$ is the correct symbol for a
          statement about all inputs, and $\Theta$ is correct only for the
          worst case over a family of inputs that actually forces the work.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A matching lower bound.</strong> Something can still be
          proved from below, and it explains why the Fibonacci-heap bound is the
          end of the road for this approach. Let $G$ be the star with edges
          <M>{tex`(s, v_i)`}</M> of weights <M>{tex`w_1, \ldots, w_{V-1}`}</M>.
          Dijkstra extracts the vertices in non-decreasing order of distance,
          i.e. it outputs the <M>{tex`w_i`}</M> in sorted order. If the priority
          queue is comparison-based, the whole run is a comparison-based sorting
          algorithm for $V-1$ numbers, so by the decision-tree bound proved for
          merge sort it must perform <M>{tex`\Omega(V \log V)`}</M> comparisons
          in the worst case. Together with the $\Omega(E)$ needed to read the
          edges,
          <E
            >{tex`\begin{equation}T \;\in\; \Omega\big(E + V \log V\big)\end{equation}`}</E
          >
          for every implementation with a comparison-based priority queue,
          which is exactly the Fibonacci-heap upper bound. In that model Dijkstra with a
          Fibonacci heap is optimal, and the binary heap's extra <M
            >{tex`\log V`}</M
          > factor on the $E$ term is the price of its cheaper decrease-key.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Which variant to use.</strong> Comparing $V^2$ with
          <M>{tex`(V+E)\log V`}</M>, the array beats the binary heap exactly
          when <M>{tex`E \in \omega(V^2/\log V)`}</M>, i.e. on dense graphs; on
          sparse graphs, where $E = O(V)$, the heap gives <M
            >{tex`O(V \log V)`}</M
          > against the array's <M>{tex`\Theta(V^2)`}</M>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Space.</strong> The arrays <M>{tex`d`}</M>, <M
            >{tex`\mathit{parent}`}</M
          >, <M>{tex`\mathit{done}`}</M> and the queue (with its index mapping vertices
          to heap positions, needed to locate a vertex for decrease-key) each
          occupy $\Theta(V)$ words, so the auxiliary space is $\Theta(V)$, on
          top of the $\Theta(V+E)$ input.
        </p>
      </section>
    </section>

    <section id="fundamental-functions">
      <h2>The Fundamental Functions of Algorithmic Analysis</h2>
      <p>
        Almost every running time met in practice is one of a dozen or so
        functions. The table below collects them in increasing order of growth,
        together with the structure that produces each one and the standard
        algorithms that realize it. Reading it downwards is a good way to
        calibrate intuition: each row is asymptotically dominated by every row
        below it, so a single step down the ladder costs more than any constant
        factor of tuning can ever recover.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table class="p-table">
          <!-- <caption
            >Growth functions of algorithmic analysis, ordered by growth
            rate.</caption
          > -->
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Growth</th>
              <th>Name</th>
              <th>Where it comes from</th>
              <th>Typical examples</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(1)$</td>
              <td>Constant</td>
              <td>
                A fixed number of primitive operations, independent of the
                input size
              </td>
              <td>
                Array indexing; fixed-width arithmetic and bitwise operations;
                assigning a primitive; stack push/pop and queue
                enqueue/dequeue; linked-list insertion at a known node;
                hash-map insert and lookup <em>on average</em> (worst case
                $\Theta(n)$ when all keys collide)
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><M>{tex`\Theta(\alpha(n))`}</M></td>
              <td>Inverse Ackermann</td>
              <td>
                Amortized cost of near-flat tree structures; a constant for
                every $n$ that fits in the universe
              </td>
              <td>
                Union-find with path compression and union by rank:
                <M>{tex`\Theta(m\,\alpha(n))`}</M> for $m$ operations on $n$
                elements
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><M>{tex`\Theta(\log^{*} n)`}</M>, $\Theta(\log\log n)$</td>
              <td>Iterated / doubly logarithmic</td>
              <td>
                Recursions that reduce the input to its logarithm at each step
              </td>
              <td>
                Interpolation search on uniform data; van Emde Boas trees;
                some specialized parallel and geometric structures
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(\log n)$</td>
              <td>Logarithmic</td>
              <td>
                Each step discards a constant fraction of the remaining input
              </td>
              <td>
                Binary search; search, insertion and deletion in a balanced
                BST (AVL, red-black, B-tree); binary-heap insert and
                extract-min; exponentiation by squaring; Euclid's GCD;
                <a
                  href="https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/fast-doubling-method-to-find-the-nth-fibonacci-number/"
                  class="url">fast-doubling</a
                > Fibonacci
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(\log^k n)$</td>
              <td>Polylogarithmic</td>
              <td>A constant number of nested logarithmic searches</td>
              <td>
                Multi-level indexing; range trees; many dynamic-connectivity
                structures
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><M>{tex`\Theta(\sqrt{n})`}</M></td>
              <td>Square root</td>
              <td>
                Block decompositions that balance <M>{tex`\sqrt{n}`}</M>
                blocks of <M>{tex`\sqrt{n}`}</M> elements
              </td>
              <td>
                Trial-division primality testing; sqrt decomposition and Mo's
                algorithm; number-theoretic sieving steps
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(n)$</td>
              <td>Linear</td>
              <td>A constant amount of work for each element, once</td>
              <td>
                Minimum/maximum of an array; linear search; traversing a
                linked list; insertion or deletion at an arbitrary position;
                comparing two arrays or strings; counting sort over a fixed
                alphabet
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(n\log n)$</td>
              <td>Linearithmic</td>
              <td>
                $\Theta(\log n)$ divide-and-conquer levels, each doing
                $\Theta(n)$ work, and the optimum for comparison sorting
              </td>
              <td>
                Merge sort, heap sort, quicksort (average case); the Fast
                Fourier Transform; sorting-based sweeps and convex hulls
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(n^2)$</td>
              <td>Quadratic</td>
              <td>
                Two nested loops over the input:
                <M>{tex`\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n} 1 = n^2`}</M>
              </td>
              <td>
                Selection and bubble sort; insertion sort in the worst case
                ($\Theta(n)$ when already sorted); naive substring search; all
                pairwise distances; Dijkstra with an array queue
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(n^3)$</td>
              <td>Cubic</td>
              <td>Three nested loops over the input</td>
              <td>
                Naive matrix multiplication (Strassen's algorithm improves it
                to <M>{tex`\Theta(n^{\log_2 7})`}</M> at the cost of a large
                constant); Floyd–Warshall all-pairs shortest paths
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(2^n)$</td>
              <td>Exponential</td>
              <td>
                One branch per binary choice: a set of $n$ elements has
                <M>{tex`\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k} = 2^n`}</M> subsets
              </td>
              <td>
                Brute-force subset sum; naive recursive Fibonacci; enumerating
                all subarrays, which is <M>{tex`\Theta(n\,2^n)`}</M>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(n!)$</td>
              <td>Factorial</td>
              <td>
                One branch per ordering of the input. By Stirling's
                approximation <M>{tex`n! = 2^{\Theta(n\log n)}`}</M>, so the
                factorial sits strictly between $\Theta(2^n)$ and
                $\Theta(n^n)$
              </td>
              <td>
                Brute-force traveling salesman; generating all permutations
                of a set
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$\Theta(n^n)$</td>
              <td>Exponential with a growing base</td>
              <td>
                Each of $n$ positions chooses independently among $n$ values,
                rather than consuming one of them as a permutation does,
                hence <M>{tex`n^n/n! \approx e^n`}</M>
              </td>
              <td>
                Enumerating all functions from an $n$-element set to itself,
                or all $n$-colorings of $n$ items; brute force over all
                labelled trees, of which there are <M>{tex`n^{n-2}`}</M>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <M>{tex`\Theta\!\left(2^{2^{n}}\right)`}</M> and beyond
              </td>
              <td>Doubly exponential, non-elementary, Ackermannian</td>
              <td>
                Decision procedures whose search space is itself exponential
                in an exponential, or past that, bounded by no fixed tower
                of exponentials at all. These are the mirror image of the
                <M>{tex`\alpha(n)`}</M> row at the top of the table
              </td>
              <td>
                Buchberger's algorithm for Gröbner bases and quantifier
                elimination over the reals (both doubly exponential); deciding
                Presburger arithmetic (triply exponential); deciding monadic
                second-order logic on strings (non-elementary); Petri-net
                reachability (Ackermann-complete)
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

